# component: input
# decisions: API-26, DOC-1, DOC-2, FOC-1, FOC-3, FOC-5, FOC-6, FOC-8, FRM-4, FOC-14
# hunks:
#   FOC-1, FOC-3, FOC-6, FOC-8: `outline-none focus-visible:border-ring focus-visible:ring-3
#     focus-visible:ring-ring/50` becomes `outline-hidden` plus `focus:border-ring/70` — the
#     text-entry border tint, on `:focus` so it fires for mouse and keyboard alike, and no glow.
#     `outline-hidden` rather than `outline-none`, so the FOC-7 forced-colours block has an
#     outline to repaint.
#   FOC-5: the invalid tint becomes `not-focus:aria-invalid:border-destructive` (and its dark
#     counterpart), so focus outranks it; the `aria-invalid` halo rings go with the rest.
#   FRM-4: `disabled:pointer-events-none` is dropped, so a disabled input stays hoverable and a
#     Tooltip can explain it. Base UI's Input has no `focusableWhenDisabled`, so the native
#     `disabled` attribute stays — the pointer half of FRM-4 only.
#   API-26: NO HUNK — the engine already implements it. Base UI's `Input` is `Field.Control`, so
#     inside a `Field` it reads the label, description and error ids and `aria-invalid` with no
#     code; `input.test.tsx` pins it.
#   FOC-14 (supersedes the FOC-3 border tint and FOC-5 above): no `focus:` border tint and no `not-
#     focus:` guard on the invalid border — the border never changes on focus (base.css's background
#     tint is the cue), and the invalid border holds in every state.
#   cn: `import { cn } from "cn"` becomes `@vegastack/design` — our npm layer owns `cn` (DOC-2).
#   formatting: the repository formatter (prettier defaults: semicolons, trailing commas)
#               reflows the file, and `registry-stamp.mjs` writes the provenance header the
#               three-copy model needs (DOC-1); those hunks carry no decision of their own.
